# MyAutoClick
An AutoHotKey script to automate a left, right, or double mouse click when the mouse cursor stops moving for a specified time period.

MyAutoClick.ini must be present in the same folder for MyAutoClick to work.  The initial MyAutoClick.ini is created by running MyAutoClick-Parameters.exe (which was compiled using the MyAutoClick-Parameters.ahk AutoHotKey script).

Initial setup:
- Run MyAutoClick-Parameters.exe
  - Enter the wait time (in milliseconds) the mouse cursor must pause before an autoclick occurs.  For example, 1000 = 1 second.
  - Enter the polling time (in milliseconds) MyAutoClick checks to see if the mouse cursor is moving or stationary.  10 should be a good initial polling time.
  - Press the hotkey combination used to toggle MyAutoClick on and off. (Examples: F10, Shift+Ctrl+Alt+S, etc.)
  - Select the type of mouse click you want (Left, Right, or Double).

After running MyAutoClick-Parameters.exe, MyAutoClick.ini should contain the following settings:
- WaitTime - How long the mouse cursor must remain stationary before a click is registered (in milliseconds).
- PollingTime - How often MyAutoClick checks for mouse cursor movement (in milliseconds).
- Hkey - Key combination used to toggle MyAutoClick on and off.
- MouseClick - Left, Right, or Double.

When first running MyAutoClick, a red mouse cursor icon will be displayed in the System Tray (which may be displayed in the hidden System Tray icon area), indicating that MyAutoClick is running but inactive.  

By pressing the hotkey (created when running MyAutoClick-Parameters.exe), the red mouse cursor icon will be replaced with a green mouse cursor icon, indicating that MyAutoClick is running and active.

To exit MyAutoClick, right-click the red or green mouse cursor icon in the System Tray and select Exit.
